What we need now is a diagnostic that uses the same amazon.aws.aws_ssm connection to the Linux EC2.
Use your existing domain-join playbook structure and temporarily add these tasks after the Linux host is added to workload_targets, or create a small diagnostic play that targets that dynamically created host:
- name: Diagnose Linux SSM become
  hosts: workload_targets
  gather_facts: false

  tasks:
    - name: Show normal SSM user
      ansible.builtin.command:
        cmd: id
      register: normal_id
      changed_when: false

    - name: Show normal user result
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        var: normal_id.stdout

    - name: Show explicit sudo result
      ansible.builtin.command:
        cmd: sudo -n id
      register: sudo_id
      changed_when: false

    - name: Show explicit sudo output
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        var: sudo_id.stdout

    - name: Show Ansible become result
      ansible.builtin.command:
        cmd: id
      become: true
      become_method: sudo
      become_user: root
      register: become_id
      changed_when: false

    - name: Show become output
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        var: become_id.stdout
Run that through the same dynamic SSM inventory setup as the domain-join JT.
Expected diagnostic:
normal_id  -> uid=1001(ssm-user)
sudo_id    -> uid=0(root)
become_id  -> ideally uid=0(root)
If become_id still shows ssm-user, then we have reproduced the real issue properly.
One more important point: amazon.aws.aws_ssm officially says remote_user/ansible_user do not control the remote identity; become_user is the intended mechanism for changing execution identity. �
